1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,230

2
00:00:05,230 --> 00:00:09,730
Before we start, I gave
the same talk at Stanford

3
00:00:09,730 --> 00:00:11,830
quite recently.

4
00:00:11,830 --> 00:00:13,520
I suggested to the
people inviting me

5
00:00:13,520 --> 00:00:15,895
I could just give one talk
and both audiences could come,

6
00:00:15,895 --> 00:00:18,100
but they would prefer it
as two separate talks.

7
00:00:18,100 --> 00:00:20,500
So if you went to
this talk recently,

8
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:22,360
I suggest you leave now.

9
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:24,052
You won't learn anything new.

10
00:00:24,052 --> 00:00:24,552
OK.

11
00:00:24,552 --> 00:00:28,520

12
00:00:28,520 --> 00:00:30,910
What I'm going to do is
combine some recent ideas

13
00:00:30,910 --> 00:00:33,895
in neural networks
to try to explain

14
00:00:33,895 --> 00:00:35,395
how a neural network
could represent

15
00:00:35,395 --> 00:00:39,850
part-whole hierarchies
without violating

16
00:00:39,850 --> 00:00:43,630
any of the basic principles
of how neurons work.

17
00:00:43,630 --> 00:00:48,100
And I'm going to explain
these ideas in terms

18
00:00:48,100 --> 00:00:50,290
of an imaginary system.

19
00:00:50,290 --> 00:00:52,390
I started writing a design
document for a system.

20
00:00:52,390 --> 00:00:54,910
And in the end, I decided
the design document by itself

21
00:00:54,910 --> 00:00:56,210
was quite interesting.

22
00:00:56,210 --> 00:00:58,753
So this is just vaporware,
stuff that doesn't exist.

23
00:00:58,753 --> 00:00:59,920
Little bits of it now exist.

24
00:00:59,920 --> 00:01:03,610
But somehow I find
it easy to explain

25
00:01:03,610 --> 00:01:05,634
the ideas in the context
of an imaginary system.

26
00:01:05,634 --> 00:01:11,170

27
00:01:11,170 --> 00:01:14,400
So most people now
studying neural networks

28
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:16,440
are doing engineering,
and they don't really

29
00:01:16,440 --> 00:01:18,660
care if it's exactly
how the brain works.

30
00:01:18,660 --> 00:01:20,868
They're not trying to
understand how the brain works.

31
00:01:20,868 --> 00:01:22,710
They're trying to
make cool technology.

32
00:01:22,710 --> 00:01:25,230
And so a hundred layers
is fine in a ResNet.

33
00:01:25,230 --> 00:01:29,040
Weight sharing is fine in
a convolutional neural net.

34
00:01:29,040 --> 00:01:32,790
Some researchers, particularly
computational neuroscientists,

35
00:01:32,790 --> 00:01:35,520
investigate artificial
neural networks

36
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:38,700
in an attempt to understand how
the brain might actually work.

37
00:01:38,700 --> 00:01:41,680
I think we still got a lot
to learn from the brain.

38
00:01:41,680 --> 00:01:43,710
And I think it's
worth remembering

39
00:01:43,710 --> 00:01:46,020
that for about half a
century, the only thing that

40
00:01:46,020 --> 00:01:48,665
kept research on neural
networks going was the belief

41
00:01:48,665 --> 00:01:50,790
that it must be possible
to make these things learn

42
00:01:50,790 --> 00:01:52,540
complicated things
because the brain does.

43
00:01:52,540 --> 00:01:55,720

44
00:01:55,720 --> 00:02:01,212
So every image has a
different parse tree

45
00:02:01,212 --> 00:02:02,670
that is the structure
of the wholes

46
00:02:02,670 --> 00:02:05,190
in the parts in the image.

47
00:02:05,190 --> 00:02:07,890
And in a real
neural network, you

48
00:02:07,890 --> 00:02:09,517
can't dynamically allocate it.

49
00:02:09,517 --> 00:02:11,100
You can't just grab
a bunch of neurons

50
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:14,880
and say, OK, you
now represent this

51
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:16,800
because you don't have
random access memory.

52
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:19,260
You can't just set the
weights of the neurons

53
00:02:19,260 --> 00:02:20,370
to be whatever you like.

54
00:02:20,370 --> 00:02:22,860
What a neuron does is
determined by its connections.

55
00:02:22,860 --> 00:02:26,250
And they only change
slowly at least probably.

56
00:02:26,250 --> 00:02:29,520
Mostly, they change slowly.

57
00:02:29,520 --> 00:02:33,150
So the question is, if you can't
change what neurons do quickly,

58
00:02:33,150 --> 00:02:35,700
how can you represent
a dynamic parse tree?

59
00:02:35,700 --> 00:02:40,450

60
00:02:40,450 --> 00:02:42,070
In symbolic AI,
it's not a problem.

61
00:02:42,070 --> 00:02:44,020
You just grab a
piece of memory--

62
00:02:44,020 --> 00:02:45,640
that's what it
normally amounts to--

63
00:02:45,640 --> 00:02:47,560
and say this is
going to represent

64
00:02:47,560 --> 00:02:48,970
a node in the
parse tree, and I'm

65
00:02:48,970 --> 00:02:50,800
going to give it
pointers to other nodes,

66
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:52,970
other bits of memory that
represent other nodes.

67
00:02:52,970 --> 00:02:54,610
So there's no problem.

68
00:02:54,610 --> 00:02:58,450
For about five years, I played
with a theory called Capsules

69
00:02:58,450 --> 00:03:03,240
where you say because you can't
allocate neurons on the fly,

70
00:03:03,240 --> 00:03:05,100
you're going to allocate
them in advance.

71
00:03:05,100 --> 00:03:06,690
So we can take
groups of neurons,

72
00:03:06,690 --> 00:03:09,190
and we're going to allocate
them to different possible nodes

73
00:03:09,190 --> 00:03:10,560
in a parse tree.

74
00:03:10,560 --> 00:03:13,620
And most of these groups
of neurons for most images

75
00:03:13,620 --> 00:03:15,090
are going to be silent.

76
00:03:15,090 --> 00:03:17,220
A few are going to be active.

77
00:03:17,220 --> 00:03:19,560
And then the ones that are
active we have to dynamically

78
00:03:19,560 --> 00:03:21,250
hook them up into a parse tree.

79
00:03:21,250 --> 00:03:24,480
So we have to have a way of
routing between these groups

80
00:03:24,480 --> 00:03:26,240
of neurons.

81
00:03:26,240 --> 00:03:28,210
So that was the Capsule theory.

82
00:03:28,210 --> 00:03:30,760
And I had some very
competent people

83
00:03:30,760 --> 00:03:34,180
working with me who
actually made it work,

84
00:03:34,180 --> 00:03:36,910
but it was tough going.

85
00:03:36,910 --> 00:03:38,650
My view is that some
ideas want to work,

86
00:03:38,650 --> 00:03:40,120
and some ideas
don't want to work

87
00:03:40,120 --> 00:03:41,830
and capsules are the
sort of in-between.

88
00:03:41,830 --> 00:03:43,750
Things like backpropagation
just want to work.

89
00:03:43,750 --> 00:03:46,010
You try them and they work.

90
00:03:46,010 --> 00:03:49,030
There's other ideas I've had
that just don't want to work.

91
00:03:49,030 --> 00:03:51,970
Capsules was sort of in
between, and we got it working.

92
00:03:51,970 --> 00:03:54,430
But I now have a new
theory that could

93
00:03:54,430 --> 00:03:57,670
be seen as a funny
kind of capsules model

94
00:03:57,670 --> 00:04:00,490
in which each capsule is
universal, that is instead

95
00:04:00,490 --> 00:04:04,600
of a capsule being dedicated
to a particular kind of thing,

96
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:09,070
each capsule can represent
any kind of thing,

97
00:04:09,070 --> 00:04:12,520
but hardware still comes
in capsules, which are also

98
00:04:12,520 --> 00:04:13,930
called embedding sometimes.

99
00:04:13,930 --> 00:04:16,839

100
00:04:16,839 --> 00:04:21,279
So the imaginary system I'll
talk about is called GLOM.

101
00:04:21,279 --> 00:04:26,160
And in GLOM hardware gets
allocated to columns.

102
00:04:26,160 --> 00:04:28,440
And each column
contains multiple levels

103
00:04:28,440 --> 00:04:30,600
of representation
of what's happening

104
00:04:30,600 --> 00:04:33,400
in a small patch of the image.

105
00:04:33,400 --> 00:04:37,360
So within a column, you
might have a lower level

106
00:04:37,360 --> 00:04:40,045
representation that
says it's a nostril.

107
00:04:40,045 --> 00:04:42,138
And the next level up
might say it's a nose.

108
00:04:42,138 --> 00:04:43,680
And the next level
up might say face,

109
00:04:43,680 --> 00:04:45,030
the next level up a person.

110
00:04:45,030 --> 00:04:47,160
And the top level
might say it's a party.

111
00:04:47,160 --> 00:04:50,070
That's what the whole scene is.

112
00:04:50,070 --> 00:04:52,710
And the idea for representing
part-whole hierarchies

113
00:04:52,710 --> 00:04:55,860
is to use islands of agreement
between the embeddings

114
00:04:55,860 --> 00:04:58,360
at these different levels.

115
00:04:58,360 --> 00:05:00,600
So at the scene level,
at the top level,

116
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:03,600
you'd like the same embedding
for every patch of the image

117
00:05:03,600 --> 00:05:07,170
because that patch is a patch
of the same scene everywhere.

118
00:05:07,170 --> 00:05:09,960
But the object level
you'd like the embeddings

119
00:05:09,960 --> 00:05:12,870
of all the different patches
that belong to the object

120
00:05:12,870 --> 00:05:14,497
to be the same.

121
00:05:14,497 --> 00:05:16,080
So as you go up this
hierarchy, you're

122
00:05:16,080 --> 00:05:18,240
trying to make things
more and more the same.

123
00:05:18,240 --> 00:05:22,500
And that's how you're
squeezing redundancy out.

124
00:05:22,500 --> 00:05:25,260
The embedding vectors are the
things that act like pointers.

125
00:05:25,260 --> 00:05:28,720
And the embedding
vectors are dynamic.

126
00:05:28,720 --> 00:05:31,300
They're neural activations
rather than neural weights.

127
00:05:31,300 --> 00:05:33,450
So it's fine to have
different embedding

128
00:05:33,450 --> 00:05:34,545
vectors for every image.

129
00:05:34,545 --> 00:05:39,130

130
00:05:39,130 --> 00:05:40,710
So here's a little picture.

131
00:05:40,710 --> 00:05:45,390
If you had a one
dimensional row of patches,

132
00:05:45,390 --> 00:05:48,460
these are the columns
for the patches.

133
00:05:48,460 --> 00:05:51,730
And you'd have something like
a convolutional neural net

134
00:05:51,730 --> 00:05:53,620
as a front end.

135
00:05:53,620 --> 00:05:56,170
And then after
the front end, you

136
00:05:56,170 --> 00:05:58,180
produce your lowest
level embeddings

137
00:05:58,180 --> 00:06:01,060
that say what's going on
in each particular patch.

138
00:06:01,060 --> 00:06:03,280
And so that bottom
layer of black arrows

139
00:06:03,280 --> 00:06:04,392
they're all different.

140
00:06:04,392 --> 00:06:06,100
Of course, these
embeddings are thousands

141
00:06:06,100 --> 00:06:10,160
of dimensions maybe hundreds
of thousands in your brain.

142
00:06:10,160 --> 00:06:14,080
And so a two-dimensional
vector isn't right.

143
00:06:14,080 --> 00:06:15,640
But at least I can
represent where

144
00:06:15,640 --> 00:06:19,030
the two vectors are the same
by using the orientation.

145
00:06:19,030 --> 00:06:21,490
So at the lowest
level, all the patches

146
00:06:21,490 --> 00:06:23,940
will have different
representations,

147
00:06:23,940 --> 00:06:27,390
but the next level up,
the first two patches,

148
00:06:27,390 --> 00:06:30,285
they might be part of
a nostril, for example.

149
00:06:30,285 --> 00:06:35,240

150
00:06:35,240 --> 00:06:37,610
And so, yeah, they'll
have the same embedding.

151
00:06:37,610 --> 00:06:40,880
But the next level up,
the first three patches

152
00:06:40,880 --> 00:06:42,920
might be part of a nose.

153
00:06:42,920 --> 00:06:45,050
And so they'll all have
the same embedding.

154
00:06:45,050 --> 00:06:47,300
Notice that even though
what's in the image

155
00:06:47,300 --> 00:06:51,640
is quite different,
at the part level,

156
00:06:51,640 --> 00:06:55,190
those three red vectors are
all meant to be the same.

157
00:06:55,190 --> 00:06:57,970
So what we're doing is we're
getting the same representation

158
00:06:57,970 --> 00:07:00,730
for things that are
superficially very different.

159
00:07:00,730 --> 00:07:03,580
We're finding spatial
coherence in an image

160
00:07:03,580 --> 00:07:04,960
by giving the same
representation

161
00:07:04,960 --> 00:07:06,820
to different things.

162
00:07:06,820 --> 00:07:10,750
And at the object level, you
might have a nose and then

163
00:07:10,750 --> 00:07:11,950
a mouth.

164
00:07:11,950 --> 00:07:13,510
And they're the same face.

165
00:07:13,510 --> 00:07:15,160
They're part of the same face.

166
00:07:15,160 --> 00:07:17,120
And so all those
vectors are the same.

167
00:07:17,120 --> 00:07:18,880
And this network
hasn't yet settled down

168
00:07:18,880 --> 00:07:22,320
to produce on the unseen level.

169
00:07:22,320 --> 00:07:27,050
So the islands of agreement are
what captured the parse tree.

170
00:07:27,050 --> 00:07:30,860
Now they're a bit more
powerful than a parse tree.

171
00:07:30,860 --> 00:07:33,830
They can capture things
like shut the heck up.

172
00:07:33,830 --> 00:07:37,580
You can have shut and up can be
different vectors at one level.

173
00:07:37,580 --> 00:07:40,010
But at a higher
level, shut and up can

174
00:07:40,010 --> 00:07:43,610
have exactly the same vector,
namely the vector for shut up,

175
00:07:43,610 --> 00:07:44,870
and they can be disconnected.

176
00:07:44,870 --> 00:07:47,390
So you can do things a bit
more powerful than the context

177
00:07:47,390 --> 00:07:48,920
free grammar here.

178
00:07:48,920 --> 00:07:51,470
But basically it's a parse tree.

179
00:07:51,470 --> 00:07:56,690
If you're a physicist, you can
think of each of these levels

180
00:07:56,690 --> 00:08:00,980
as an Ising model with real
valued vectors rather than

181
00:08:00,980 --> 00:08:02,840
binary spins.

182
00:08:02,840 --> 00:08:05,090
Then you can think of them
being coordinate transforms

183
00:08:05,090 --> 00:08:08,310
between levels, which makes
it much more complicated.

184
00:08:08,310 --> 00:08:11,360
And then this is a kind
of multi-level Ising

185
00:08:11,360 --> 00:08:15,590
model but with complicated
interactions between the levels

186
00:08:15,590 --> 00:08:17,660
because, for example,
between the red arrows

187
00:08:17,660 --> 00:08:19,610
and the black arrows
above them, you

188
00:08:19,610 --> 00:08:23,390
need the coordinate transform
between a nose and a face.

189
00:08:23,390 --> 00:08:26,330
But we'll come to that later.

190
00:08:26,330 --> 00:08:28,175
If you're not a
physicist, ignore all

191
00:08:28,175 --> 00:08:29,300
that because it won't help.

192
00:08:29,300 --> 00:08:33,240

193
00:08:33,240 --> 00:08:35,184
So I want to start on this--

194
00:08:35,184 --> 00:08:37,559
I guess it's particularly
relevant for a natural language

195
00:08:37,559 --> 00:08:42,450
course where you-- some of you
are not vision people by trying

196
00:08:42,450 --> 00:08:45,780
to prove to you that coordinate
systems are not just something

197
00:08:45,780 --> 00:08:48,540
invented by Descartes.

198
00:08:48,540 --> 00:08:53,010
Coordinate systems were invented
by the brain a long time ago.

199
00:08:53,010 --> 00:08:55,920
And we use coordinate
systems in understanding

200
00:08:55,920 --> 00:08:58,050
what's going on in an image.

201
00:08:58,050 --> 00:09:00,480
I also want to demonstrate
the psychological reality

202
00:09:00,480 --> 00:09:02,800
of parse trees for an image.

203
00:09:02,800 --> 00:09:05,460
So I'm going to do
this with a task

204
00:09:05,460 --> 00:09:09,330
that I invented a long
time ago in the 1970s

205
00:09:09,330 --> 00:09:13,100
when I was a grad
student in fact.

206
00:09:13,100 --> 00:09:16,250
And you have to do this task to
get the full benefit from it.

207
00:09:16,250 --> 00:09:19,130

208
00:09:19,130 --> 00:09:22,810
So I want you to imagine on
the tabletop in front of you

209
00:09:22,810 --> 00:09:24,970
there's a wire-frame cube.

210
00:09:24,970 --> 00:09:27,580
And it's in the standard
orientation for a cube.

211
00:09:27,580 --> 00:09:29,560
It's resting on the tabletop.

212
00:09:29,560 --> 00:09:32,350
And from your point
of view, there's

213
00:09:32,350 --> 00:09:37,060
a front bottom right hand
corner and a top back left hand

214
00:09:37,060 --> 00:09:37,630
corner.

215
00:09:37,630 --> 00:09:38,670
Here we go.

216
00:09:38,670 --> 00:09:40,870
OK.

217
00:09:40,870 --> 00:09:42,490
The front bottom
right hand corner

218
00:09:42,490 --> 00:09:46,180
is resting on the tabletop along
with the four other corners.

219
00:09:46,180 --> 00:09:48,460
And the top back
left hand corner

220
00:09:48,460 --> 00:09:50,620
is at the other end of
a diagonal, which goes

221
00:09:50,620 --> 00:09:52,886
through the center of the cube.

222
00:09:52,886 --> 00:09:54,920
OK, so far so good.

223
00:09:54,920 --> 00:09:56,900
Now, what we're going
to do is rotate the cube

224
00:09:56,900 --> 00:09:59,510
so that this finger
stays on the tabletop

225
00:09:59,510 --> 00:10:03,460
and the other finger is
vertically above it, like that.

226
00:10:03,460 --> 00:10:05,616
This finger
shouldn't have moved.

227
00:10:05,616 --> 00:10:06,640
OK.

228
00:10:06,640 --> 00:10:08,680
So now we've got the
cube in an orientation

229
00:10:08,680 --> 00:10:12,070
where that thing that was a
body diagonal is now vertical.

230
00:10:12,070 --> 00:10:15,220
And all you've got to do
is take the bottom finger

231
00:10:15,220 --> 00:10:16,902
because that's still
on the table top

232
00:10:16,902 --> 00:10:19,360
and point with the bottom finger
to where the other corners

233
00:10:19,360 --> 00:10:20,855
of the cube are.

234
00:10:20,855 --> 00:10:21,980
So I want you to try do it.

235
00:10:21,980 --> 00:10:22,640
Off you go.

236
00:10:22,640 --> 00:10:24,200
Take your bottom finger.

237
00:10:24,200 --> 00:10:26,900
Hold your top finger
at the other end

238
00:10:26,900 --> 00:10:29,480
of that diagonal that's now been
made vertical and just point

239
00:10:29,480 --> 00:10:32,940
to where the other corners are.

240
00:10:32,940 --> 00:10:37,432
And luckily it zooms
so most of you--

241
00:10:37,432 --> 00:10:39,390
other people won't be
able to see what you did.

242
00:10:39,390 --> 00:10:41,348
And I can see that some
of you aren't pointing,

243
00:10:41,348 --> 00:10:43,110
and that's very bad.

244
00:10:43,110 --> 00:10:47,400
So most people point
out four of the corners.

245
00:10:47,400 --> 00:10:50,260
And the most common response
is to say they're here, here,

246
00:10:50,260 --> 00:10:51,120
here, and here.

247
00:10:51,120 --> 00:10:54,150
They point out four corners in
a square halfway up that axis.

248
00:10:54,150 --> 00:10:57,510

249
00:10:57,510 --> 00:10:59,880
That's wrong as
you might imagine.

250
00:10:59,880 --> 00:11:01,800
And it's easy to
see that it's wrong

251
00:11:01,800 --> 00:11:04,770
because if you imagine the
cube in the normal orientation

252
00:11:04,770 --> 00:11:08,260
and count the corners,
there's eight of them.

253
00:11:08,260 --> 00:11:09,990
And these were two corners.

254
00:11:09,990 --> 00:11:12,850
So where did the
other two corners go?

255
00:11:12,850 --> 00:11:15,550
So one theory is that
when you rotated the cube,

256
00:11:15,550 --> 00:11:19,450
the centripetal forces made them
fly off into your unconscious.

257
00:11:19,450 --> 00:11:21,200
That's not a very good theory.

258
00:11:21,200 --> 00:11:24,792
So what's happening
here is you have

259
00:11:24,792 --> 00:11:27,250
no idea where the other corner,
are unless you're something

260
00:11:27,250 --> 00:11:29,350
like a crystallographer.

261
00:11:29,350 --> 00:11:31,120
You can sort of imagine
bits of the cube,

262
00:11:31,120 --> 00:11:33,745
but you just can't imagine this
structure of the other corners,

263
00:11:33,745 --> 00:11:36,480
what structure they form.

264
00:11:36,480 --> 00:11:38,400
And this common
response that people

265
00:11:38,400 --> 00:11:44,760
give of four corners in a square
is doing something very weird.

266
00:11:44,760 --> 00:11:46,590
It's saying, well, OK.

267
00:11:46,590 --> 00:11:48,780
I don't know where the
bits of the cube are,

268
00:11:48,780 --> 00:11:50,250
but I know something
about cubes.

269
00:11:50,250 --> 00:11:52,470
I know the corners
come in fours.

270
00:11:52,470 --> 00:11:56,640
I know a cube has this four-fold
rotational symmetry or two

271
00:11:56,640 --> 00:12:00,070
planes of bilateral symmetry
but right angles to one another.

272
00:12:00,070 --> 00:12:02,970
And so what people do is
they preserve the symmetries

273
00:12:02,970 --> 00:12:04,950
of the cube in their response.

274
00:12:04,950 --> 00:12:08,430
They give four
corners in a square.

275
00:12:08,430 --> 00:12:10,230
Now, what they've
actually pointed out

276
00:12:10,230 --> 00:12:14,550
if they do that is two
pyramids each of which

277
00:12:14,550 --> 00:12:15,960
has a square base.

278
00:12:15,960 --> 00:12:19,560
One's upside down, and
they're stuck base to base.

279
00:12:19,560 --> 00:12:21,670
So you can visualize
that quite easily.

280
00:12:21,670 --> 00:12:23,610
So square base pyramid
with another one

281
00:12:23,610 --> 00:12:25,242
stuck underneath it.

282
00:12:25,242 --> 00:12:26,700
And so now you get
your two fingers

283
00:12:26,700 --> 00:12:29,400
as the vertices of
those two pyramids.

284
00:12:29,400 --> 00:12:32,220
And what's
interesting about that

285
00:12:32,220 --> 00:12:36,240
is you've preserved the
symmetries of the cube

286
00:12:36,240 --> 00:12:39,300
at the cost of doing something
pretty radical, which

287
00:12:39,300 --> 00:12:44,370
is changing faces to vertices
and vertices to faces.

288
00:12:44,370 --> 00:12:48,790
The thing you pointed out if
you did that was an octahedron.

289
00:12:48,790 --> 00:12:51,460
It has eight faces
and 6 vertices.

290
00:12:51,460 --> 00:12:54,260
A cube has six faces
and eight vertices.

291
00:12:54,260 --> 00:12:56,230
So in order to
preserve the symmetries

292
00:12:56,230 --> 00:12:59,870
you know about of
the cube, if you

293
00:12:59,870 --> 00:13:03,230
did that, you've done
something really radical, which

294
00:13:03,230 --> 00:13:07,850
has changed faces for vertices
and vertices for faces.

295
00:13:07,850 --> 00:13:09,990
I should show you what
the answer looks like.

296
00:13:09,990 --> 00:13:13,460
So I'm going to step back
and try and get enough light

297
00:13:13,460 --> 00:13:15,290
and maybe you can see this cube.

298
00:13:15,290 --> 00:13:18,326

299
00:13:18,326 --> 00:13:21,440
So this is a cube.

300
00:13:21,440 --> 00:13:26,510
And you can see
that the other edges

301
00:13:26,510 --> 00:13:30,040
form a kind of zig-zag
ring around the middle.

302
00:13:30,040 --> 00:13:31,570
So I got a picture of it.

303
00:13:31,570 --> 00:13:34,580

304
00:13:34,580 --> 00:13:37,430
So the colored rods
here are the other edges

305
00:13:37,430 --> 00:13:40,340
of the cube, the ones that
don't touch your fingertips.

306
00:13:40,340 --> 00:13:42,860
And your top fingers are
connected to the three vertices

307
00:13:42,860 --> 00:13:45,830
of those flaps when
your bottom finger's

308
00:13:45,830 --> 00:13:49,693
connected to the lowest
three vertices there.

309
00:13:49,693 --> 00:13:51,110
And that's what a
cube looks like.

310
00:13:51,110 --> 00:13:53,270
It's something you
had no idea about.

311
00:13:53,270 --> 00:13:55,880
This is just a completely
different model of a cube.

312
00:13:55,880 --> 00:13:56,670
It's so different.

313
00:13:56,670 --> 00:13:57,920
I'll give it a different name.

314
00:13:57,920 --> 00:13:59,940
I'll call it a hexahedron.

315
00:13:59,940 --> 00:14:03,840
And the thing to
notice is a hexahedron

316
00:14:03,840 --> 00:14:06,300
and a cube are
just conceptually,

317
00:14:06,300 --> 00:14:07,410
utterly different.

318
00:14:07,410 --> 00:14:09,970
You wouldn't even know one
was the same as the other

319
00:14:09,970 --> 00:14:12,480
if you think about one as a
hexahedron and one as a cube.

320
00:14:12,480 --> 00:14:15,510
It's like the ambiguity
between a tilted square

321
00:14:15,510 --> 00:14:17,760
and an upright diamond
but more powerful

322
00:14:17,760 --> 00:14:21,000
because you're not
familiar with it.

323
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,070
And that's my demonstration
that people really

324
00:14:23,070 --> 00:14:24,417
do use coordinate systems.

325
00:14:24,417 --> 00:14:26,250
And if you use a different
coordinate system

326
00:14:26,250 --> 00:14:27,390
to describe things--

327
00:14:27,390 --> 00:14:29,880
and here I forced you to use
a different coordinate system

328
00:14:29,880 --> 00:14:32,280
by making the
diagonal be vertical

329
00:14:32,280 --> 00:14:35,190
and asking you to describe it
relative to that vertical axis.

330
00:14:35,190 --> 00:14:39,200
Then familiar things become
completely unfamiliar.

331
00:14:39,200 --> 00:14:41,450
And when you do see them
relative to this new frame,

332
00:14:41,450 --> 00:14:44,250
they just are a completely
different thing.

333
00:14:44,250 --> 00:14:46,440
Notice that things like
convolutional neural nets

334
00:14:46,440 --> 00:14:47,298
don't have that.

335
00:14:47,298 --> 00:14:48,840
They can't look at
something and have

336
00:14:48,840 --> 00:14:50,880
two utterly different
internal representations

337
00:14:50,880 --> 00:14:53,350
of the very same thing.

338
00:14:53,350 --> 00:14:55,900
I'm also showing you
that you do parsing.

339
00:14:55,900 --> 00:14:57,760
So here I've colored
it so you parse it

340
00:14:57,760 --> 00:15:01,180
into what I call the crown,
which is three triangular

341
00:15:01,180 --> 00:15:04,040
flaps that slope
upwards and outwards.

342
00:15:04,040 --> 00:15:07,250
Here's a different parsing,
the same green flaps

343
00:15:07,250 --> 00:15:08,970
sloping upwards and outwards.

344
00:15:08,970 --> 00:15:12,500
Now, we have a red flap
sloping downwards and outwards.

345
00:15:12,500 --> 00:15:14,450
And we have a central rectangle.

346
00:15:14,450 --> 00:15:17,190
And you just have the two
ends of the rectangle.

347
00:15:17,190 --> 00:15:21,920
And if you perceive this, then
I close your eyes and ask you,

348
00:15:21,920 --> 00:15:24,410
were there any
parallel edges there?

349
00:15:24,410 --> 00:15:27,230
You're very well aware
that those two blue edges

350
00:15:27,230 --> 00:15:28,460
were parallel.

351
00:15:28,460 --> 00:15:31,160
Then you're typically not aware
of any other parallel edges

352
00:15:31,160 --> 00:15:34,190
even though you know by
symmetry there must be pairs.

353
00:15:34,190 --> 00:15:35,720
Similarly with the crown.

354
00:15:35,720 --> 00:15:38,930
If you see the crown and then
I ask you to close your eyes

355
00:15:38,930 --> 00:15:40,880
and ask you where
the parallel edge is,

356
00:15:40,880 --> 00:15:43,370
you don't see any
parallel edges.

357
00:15:43,370 --> 00:15:45,050
And that's because
the coordinate system

358
00:15:45,050 --> 00:15:48,680
you're using for those flaps
don't line up with the edges.

359
00:15:48,680 --> 00:15:50,630
Then you only notice
parallel edges

360
00:15:50,630 --> 00:15:53,360
if they line up with the
coordinate system you see.

361
00:15:53,360 --> 00:15:55,850
So here for the rectangle,
our parallel edges

362
00:15:55,850 --> 00:15:57,350
align with the
coordinate system.

363
00:15:57,350 --> 00:15:58,910
For the flaps, they don't.

364
00:15:58,910 --> 00:16:01,280
So you're aware that those
two blue edges are parallel,

365
00:16:01,280 --> 00:16:03,650
but you're not aware that one
of the green edges and one

366
00:16:03,650 --> 00:16:04,900
of the red edges are parallel.

367
00:16:04,900 --> 00:16:10,460

368
00:16:10,460 --> 00:16:12,550
So this isn't like the
Necker cube ambiguity

369
00:16:12,550 --> 00:16:15,220
where when it flips you
think that what's out there

370
00:16:15,220 --> 00:16:16,450
in reality is different.

371
00:16:16,450 --> 00:16:18,220
Things are at a different depth.

372
00:16:18,220 --> 00:16:22,447
This is like, next weekend we
should be visiting relatives.

373
00:16:22,447 --> 00:16:24,280
So if you take the
sentence "next weekend we

374
00:16:24,280 --> 00:16:27,310
should be visiting
relatives," it can mean,

375
00:16:27,310 --> 00:16:31,420
next weekend what we will be
doing is visiting relatives,

376
00:16:31,420 --> 00:16:34,870
or it can mean, next
weekend what we will be

377
00:16:34,870 --> 00:16:37,150
is visiting relatives.

378
00:16:37,150 --> 00:16:39,370
Now those are completely
different senses.

379
00:16:39,370 --> 00:16:41,590
They happen to have the
same truth conditions.

380
00:16:41,590 --> 00:16:44,950
They mean the same thing in
the sense of truth conditions

381
00:16:44,950 --> 00:16:47,410
because if you're visiting
relatives, what you are

382
00:16:47,410 --> 00:16:49,000
is visiting relatives.

383
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:50,397
And it's that kind of ambiguity.

384
00:16:50,397 --> 00:16:52,480
No disagreement about
what's going on in the world

385
00:16:52,480 --> 00:16:54,855
but two completely different
ways of seeing the sentence.

386
00:16:54,855 --> 00:17:01,640

387
00:17:01,640 --> 00:17:03,870
This was drawn in the 1970s.

388
00:17:03,870 --> 00:17:08,520
This is what AI was
like in the 1970s.

389
00:17:08,520 --> 00:17:11,480
This is a sort of structural
description of the crown

390
00:17:11,480 --> 00:17:13,560
interpretation.

391
00:17:13,560 --> 00:17:17,780
So you have nodes for all
various parts in the hierarchy.

392
00:17:17,780 --> 00:17:19,849
I've also put
something on the arcs.

393
00:17:19,849 --> 00:17:26,359
That Rwx is the relationship
between the crown and the flap.

394
00:17:26,359 --> 00:17:28,460
And that can be
represented by a matrix.

395
00:17:28,460 --> 00:17:31,310
It's really the relationship
between the intrinsic frame

396
00:17:31,310 --> 00:17:33,800
of reference of the crown
and the intrinsic frame

397
00:17:33,800 --> 00:17:35,660
of reference of the flap.

398
00:17:35,660 --> 00:17:38,960
Then notice that if I
change my viewpoint,

399
00:17:38,960 --> 00:17:41,427
that doesn't change at all.

400
00:17:41,427 --> 00:17:43,510
So that kind of relationship
would be a good thing

401
00:17:43,510 --> 00:17:45,622
to put in the weights
of a neural network

402
00:17:45,622 --> 00:17:47,080
because you like
the neural network

403
00:17:47,080 --> 00:17:50,320
to be able to recognize shapes
independently of viewpoint.

404
00:17:50,320 --> 00:17:53,800
And that Rwx is knowledge
about the shape that's

405
00:17:53,800 --> 00:17:57,070
independent of viewpoint.

406
00:17:57,070 --> 00:18:00,520
Here's the "zig-zag"
interpretation.

407
00:18:00,520 --> 00:18:04,210
And here's something else
where I've added the things

408
00:18:04,210 --> 00:18:06,280
in the heavy blue boxes.

409
00:18:06,280 --> 00:18:09,970
They're the relationship
between the--

410
00:18:09,970 --> 00:18:13,810
a node and the viewer, that
is to be more explicit.

411
00:18:13,810 --> 00:18:16,630
The coordinate transformation
between the intrinsic frame

412
00:18:16,630 --> 00:18:19,270
of reference of the crown
and the intrinsic frame

413
00:18:19,270 --> 00:18:24,400
of reference of the viewer,
your eyeball is that Rwv.

414
00:18:24,400 --> 00:18:25,900
And that's a different
kind of thing

415
00:18:25,900 --> 00:18:29,600
altogether because as you
change viewpoint, that changes.

416
00:18:29,600 --> 00:18:31,000
In fact, as you
change viewpoint,

417
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:33,070
all those things
in blue boxes all

418
00:18:33,070 --> 00:18:36,340
change together in
a consistent way.

419
00:18:36,340 --> 00:18:37,850
And there's a
simple relationship,

420
00:18:37,850 --> 00:18:42,220
which is that if you take Rwv
then you multiply it by Rwx,

421
00:18:42,220 --> 00:18:44,410
you get Rxv.

422
00:18:44,410 --> 00:18:47,590
So you can easily propagate
viewpoint information

423
00:18:47,590 --> 00:18:49,980
over a structural description.

424
00:18:49,980 --> 00:18:52,490
And that's what I think
a mental image is.

425
00:18:52,490 --> 00:18:55,520
Rather than a bunch
of pixels, it's

426
00:18:55,520 --> 00:18:58,190
a structural description
with associated viewpoint

427
00:18:58,190 --> 00:18:59,000
information.

428
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:02,230

429
00:19:02,230 --> 00:19:04,930
That makes sense of a lot of
properties of mental images,

430
00:19:04,930 --> 00:19:09,640
like if you want to do any
reasoning with things like Rwx,

431
00:19:09,640 --> 00:19:13,425
you form a mental image,
that is you fill in

432
00:19:13,425 --> 00:19:15,665
or you choose a viewpoint.

433
00:19:15,665 --> 00:19:18,040
And I want to do one more demo
to convince you you always

434
00:19:18,040 --> 00:19:20,248
choose a viewpoint when
you're solving mental imagery

435
00:19:20,248 --> 00:19:21,072
problems.

436
00:19:21,072 --> 00:19:23,530
So I'm going to give you another
very simple mental imagery

437
00:19:23,530 --> 00:19:24,340
problem.

438
00:19:24,340 --> 00:19:27,610
At the risk of
running over time.

439
00:19:27,610 --> 00:19:31,640
Imagine that you're
at a particular point

440
00:19:31,640 --> 00:19:35,080
and you travel a mile east, and
then you travel a mile north,

441
00:19:35,080 --> 00:19:37,390
and then you travel
a mile east again.

442
00:19:37,390 --> 00:19:40,790
What's your direction back
to your starting point?

443
00:19:40,790 --> 00:19:42,990
This isn't a very hard problem.

444
00:19:42,990 --> 00:19:46,310
It's sort of a bit South
and quite a lot west, right?

445
00:19:46,310 --> 00:19:50,480
It's not exactly southwest,
but it's sort of southwest.

446
00:19:50,480 --> 00:19:54,380
Now, when you did that
task, what you imagined

447
00:19:54,380 --> 00:19:57,243
from your point of view
is you went a mile east,

448
00:19:57,243 --> 00:19:58,910
and then you went a
mile north, and then

449
00:19:58,910 --> 00:20:01,490
you went a mile east again.

450
00:20:01,490 --> 00:20:03,170
I'll tell you what
you didn't imagine.

451
00:20:03,170 --> 00:20:05,120
You didn't imagine that
you went a mile east,

452
00:20:05,120 --> 00:20:06,578
and you went a mile
north, and then

453
00:20:06,578 --> 00:20:07,850
you went a mile east again.

454
00:20:07,850 --> 00:20:09,642
You could have solved
the problem perfectly

455
00:20:09,642 --> 00:20:11,240
well with north not being up.

456
00:20:11,240 --> 00:20:12,860
But you had north up.

457
00:20:12,860 --> 00:20:14,323
You also didn't imagine this.

458
00:20:14,323 --> 00:20:16,490
You got a mile east, and
then a mile north, and then

459
00:20:16,490 --> 00:20:17,603
a mile east again.

460
00:20:17,603 --> 00:20:18,770
And you didn't imagine this.

461
00:20:18,770 --> 00:20:21,170
You go a mile east, and
then a mile north and so on.

462
00:20:21,170 --> 00:20:23,330
You imagined it at
a particular scale

463
00:20:23,330 --> 00:20:25,775
in a particular orientation
and in a particular position.

464
00:20:25,775 --> 00:20:28,420

465
00:20:28,420 --> 00:20:30,910
And you can answer questions
about roughly how big

466
00:20:30,910 --> 00:20:31,940
it was and so on.

467
00:20:31,940 --> 00:20:35,740
So that's evidence that
to solve these tasks that

468
00:20:35,740 --> 00:20:39,070
involve using relationships
between things,

469
00:20:39,070 --> 00:20:41,724
you form a mental image.

470
00:20:41,724 --> 00:20:46,030
OK, enough on mental imagery.

471
00:20:46,030 --> 00:20:48,310
So I'm now going to give you
a very brief introduction

472
00:20:48,310 --> 00:20:50,740
to contrastive
learning, where this

473
00:20:50,740 --> 00:20:54,130
is a complete
disconnect in the talk,

474
00:20:54,130 --> 00:20:57,520
but they'll come
back together soon.

475
00:20:57,520 --> 00:21:02,330
So in contrast to
self-supervised learning, what

476
00:21:02,330 --> 00:21:06,530
we try and do is make two
different crops of an image

477
00:21:06,530 --> 00:21:08,360
have the same representation.

478
00:21:08,360 --> 00:21:12,080

479
00:21:12,080 --> 00:21:14,290
There's a paper a long time
ago by Becker and Hinton

480
00:21:14,290 --> 00:21:17,590
where we're doing this to
discover low-level coherence

481
00:21:17,590 --> 00:21:20,890
in an image, like the
continuity of surfaces

482
00:21:20,890 --> 00:21:25,750
or the depth of surfaces.

483
00:21:25,750 --> 00:21:27,370
It's been improved
a lot since then.

484
00:21:27,370 --> 00:21:30,160
And it's been used for doing
things like classification.

485
00:21:30,160 --> 00:21:36,550
That is you take an image that
has one prominent object in it

486
00:21:36,550 --> 00:21:40,900
and you say if I take a crop
of the image that contains sort

487
00:21:40,900 --> 00:21:44,192
of any part of that
object, it should

488
00:21:44,192 --> 00:21:46,150
have the same representation
as some other crop

489
00:21:46,150 --> 00:21:49,310
of the image containing
a part of that object.

490
00:21:49,310 --> 00:21:54,010
And this has been developed
a lot in the last few years.

491
00:21:54,010 --> 00:21:55,930
I'm going to talk
about a model developed

492
00:21:55,930 --> 00:21:58,900
a couple of years ago by my
group in Toronto called SimCLR.

493
00:21:58,900 --> 00:22:00,613
But there's lots
of other models.

494
00:22:00,613 --> 00:22:02,155
And since then,
things have improved.

495
00:22:02,155 --> 00:22:05,520

496
00:22:05,520 --> 00:22:08,750
So in SimCLR, you
take an image x.

497
00:22:08,750 --> 00:22:11,810
You take two different crops.

498
00:22:11,810 --> 00:22:15,200
And you also do color distortion
of the crops, different color

499
00:22:15,200 --> 00:22:16,980
distortions of each crop.

500
00:22:16,980 --> 00:22:19,340
And that's to prevent it
from using color histograms

501
00:22:19,340 --> 00:22:21,750
to say they're the same.

502
00:22:21,750 --> 00:22:24,410
So you mess with the color
so it can't use color

503
00:22:24,410 --> 00:22:27,120
in a simple way.

504
00:22:27,120 --> 00:22:32,550
And that gives you xi
tilde, and xj tilde.

505
00:22:32,550 --> 00:22:36,420
You then put those through
the same neural network, f.

506
00:22:36,420 --> 00:22:39,445
Then you get a representation h.

507
00:22:39,445 --> 00:22:41,070
And then you take
the representation h,

508
00:22:41,070 --> 00:22:42,987
and you put it through
another neural network,

509
00:22:42,987 --> 00:22:44,630
which compresses it a bit.

510
00:22:44,630 --> 00:22:47,243
And it goes to low
dimensionality.

511
00:22:47,243 --> 00:22:49,410
That's an extra complexity
I'm not going to explain,

512
00:22:49,410 --> 00:22:51,790
but it makes it
work a bit better.

513
00:22:51,790 --> 00:22:53,520
You can do it
without doing that.

514
00:22:53,520 --> 00:22:57,130
And you get two
embeddings, zi and zj.

515
00:22:57,130 --> 00:22:59,530
And your aim is to
maximize the agreement

516
00:22:59,530 --> 00:23:02,187
between those vectors.

517
00:23:02,187 --> 00:23:03,520
And so you start off doing that.

518
00:23:03,520 --> 00:23:07,310
And you say, OK, let's start
off with random neural networks,

519
00:23:07,310 --> 00:23:09,040
random weights in
the neural networks.

520
00:23:09,040 --> 00:23:10,450
And let's take two patches.

521
00:23:10,450 --> 00:23:12,646
And let's put them through
these transformations.

522
00:23:12,646 --> 00:23:15,490
And let's try and make
zi be the same as zj.

523
00:23:15,490 --> 00:23:17,950
So let's back propagate
the squared difference

524
00:23:17,950 --> 00:23:21,100
between components of
i and components of j.

525
00:23:21,100 --> 00:23:23,410
And hey, presto,
what you discover

526
00:23:23,410 --> 00:23:29,120
is when everything
collapses, for every image,

527
00:23:29,120 --> 00:23:31,997
it will always produce
the same zi and zj.

528
00:23:31,997 --> 00:23:33,580
And then you realize,
well, that's not

529
00:23:33,580 --> 00:23:34,900
what I meant by agreement.

530
00:23:34,900 --> 00:23:37,510
I mean, they should
be the same when

531
00:23:37,510 --> 00:23:40,030
you get two crops of the
same image and different

532
00:23:40,030 --> 00:23:42,362
when you get two crops
with different images.

533
00:23:42,362 --> 00:23:44,320
Otherwise, there's not
really agreement, right?

534
00:23:44,320 --> 00:23:48,580

535
00:23:48,580 --> 00:23:50,830
So you have to have
negative examples.

536
00:23:50,830 --> 00:23:53,350
You have to show it crops
from different images

537
00:23:53,350 --> 00:23:55,540
and say those
should be different.

538
00:23:55,540 --> 00:23:57,670
If they're already
different, you

539
00:23:57,670 --> 00:24:00,010
don't try and make them
a lot more different.

540
00:24:00,010 --> 00:24:02,110
It's very easy to make
things very different,

541
00:24:02,110 --> 00:24:03,380
but that's not what you want.

542
00:24:03,380 --> 00:24:05,422
You just want to be sure
they're different enough

543
00:24:05,422 --> 00:24:07,840
so crops from different
images aren't taken

544
00:24:07,840 --> 00:24:09,350
to be from the same image.

545
00:24:09,350 --> 00:24:12,160
So if they happen to be very
similar, you push them apart.

546
00:24:12,160 --> 00:24:14,230
And that stops your
representations collapsing.

547
00:24:14,230 --> 00:24:16,000
That's called
contrastive learning.

548
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:18,650
And it works very well.

549
00:24:18,650 --> 00:24:23,470
So what you can do is
do unsupervised learning

550
00:24:23,470 --> 00:24:28,000
by trying to maximize agreement
between the representations you

551
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:31,370
get from two image patches
from the same image.

552
00:24:31,370 --> 00:24:33,070
And after you've
done that, you just

553
00:24:33,070 --> 00:24:36,220
take your representation
of the image patch,

554
00:24:36,220 --> 00:24:39,250
and you feed it to a linear
classifier, a bunch of weights

555
00:24:39,250 --> 00:24:41,680
so that you multiply the
representation by a weight

556
00:24:41,680 --> 00:24:45,640
matrix, put it through a
softmax and get close labels.

557
00:24:45,640 --> 00:24:48,700
And then you train that
by gradient descent.

558
00:24:48,700 --> 00:24:53,440
And what you discover is
that that's just about as

559
00:24:53,440 --> 00:24:55,970
good as training on label data.

560
00:24:55,970 --> 00:24:58,090
So now the only thing you
trained on labeled data

561
00:24:58,090 --> 00:25:00,310
is that last linear classifier.

562
00:25:00,310 --> 00:25:03,460
The previous slides were
trained on unlabeled data.

563
00:25:03,460 --> 00:25:07,062
And you managed to train
your representations

564
00:25:07,062 --> 00:25:08,020
without needing labels.

565
00:25:08,020 --> 00:25:11,930

566
00:25:11,930 --> 00:25:15,270
Now, there's a
problem with this.

567
00:25:15,270 --> 00:25:18,560
It works very nicely, but
it's really confounding

568
00:25:18,560 --> 00:25:21,530
objects and whole scenes.

569
00:25:21,530 --> 00:25:23,720
So it makes sense to say
two different patches

570
00:25:23,720 --> 00:25:28,220
from the same scene
should get the same vector

571
00:25:28,220 --> 00:25:33,055
label at the scene level because
they're from the same scene.

572
00:25:33,055 --> 00:25:34,680
But what if one of
the patches contains

573
00:25:34,680 --> 00:25:36,722
bits of objects A and B
and another patch contain

574
00:25:36,722 --> 00:25:37,945
bits of objects A and C?

575
00:25:37,945 --> 00:25:39,570
You don't really want
those two patches

576
00:25:39,570 --> 00:25:42,520
to have the same representation
at the object level.

577
00:25:42,520 --> 00:25:44,610
So we have to distinguish
these different levels

578
00:25:44,610 --> 00:25:46,700
of representation.

579
00:25:46,700 --> 00:25:49,610
And for contrastive
learning, if you

580
00:25:49,610 --> 00:25:53,000
don't use any kind of
gating or attention,

581
00:25:53,000 --> 00:25:55,223
then what's happening
is you're really doing

582
00:25:55,223 --> 00:25:56,390
learning at the scene level.

583
00:25:56,390 --> 00:25:59,160

584
00:25:59,160 --> 00:26:02,130
What we'd like is that
the representations you

585
00:26:02,130 --> 00:26:07,740
get at the object level should
be the same if both patches are

586
00:26:07,740 --> 00:26:09,930
patches of object A
but should be different

587
00:26:09,930 --> 00:26:12,493
if one patch is for object A
and one patch is for object B.

588
00:26:12,493 --> 00:26:14,910
And to do that, we're going
to need some form of attention

589
00:26:14,910 --> 00:26:17,960
to decide whether they really
come from the same thing.

590
00:26:17,960 --> 00:26:19,510
And so GLOM is
designed to do that.

591
00:26:19,510 --> 00:26:21,940
It's trying to take
contrastive learning

592
00:26:21,940 --> 00:26:24,340
and to introduce a
tension of the kind you

593
00:26:24,340 --> 00:26:27,940
get in transformers in order
not to try and say things

594
00:26:27,940 --> 00:26:31,330
are the same when they're not.

595
00:26:31,330 --> 00:26:33,700
I should mention at this
point that most of you

596
00:26:33,700 --> 00:26:35,830
will be familiar with BERT.

597
00:26:35,830 --> 00:26:37,690
Then you can think
of the word fragments

598
00:26:37,690 --> 00:26:39,220
that are fed into BERT.

599
00:26:39,220 --> 00:26:42,310
There's like the image
patches I'm using here.

600
00:26:42,310 --> 00:26:45,130
And in BERT, you have that
whole column of representations

601
00:26:45,130 --> 00:26:47,740
of the same word fragment.

602
00:26:47,740 --> 00:26:50,680
In BERT, what's happening
presumably as you go up

603
00:26:50,680 --> 00:26:56,440
is you're getting semantically
richer representations.

604
00:26:56,440 --> 00:27:00,370
But in BERT, there's no
attempt to get representations

605
00:27:00,370 --> 00:27:02,095
of larger things
like whole phrases.

606
00:27:02,095 --> 00:27:05,470

607
00:27:05,470 --> 00:27:08,230
What I'm going to talk about
will be a way to modify BERT So

608
00:27:08,230 --> 00:27:11,920
as you go up, you get bigger
and bigger islands of agreement.

609
00:27:11,920 --> 00:27:15,550
So, for example, after
a couple of levels,

610
00:27:15,550 --> 00:27:18,640
then things like
"New" and "York"

611
00:27:18,640 --> 00:27:21,415
will have the different
fragments of "York."

612
00:27:21,415 --> 00:27:23,710
And suppose its got two
different fragments will

613
00:27:23,710 --> 00:27:25,570
have exactly the same
representation if it

614
00:27:25,570 --> 00:27:27,460
was done in the GLOM-like way.

615
00:27:27,460 --> 00:27:29,770
And then as you go
up another level,

616
00:27:29,770 --> 00:27:31,840
the fragments of "New"
or new's probably

617
00:27:31,840 --> 00:27:34,840
a theme in its own right
and the fragments of "York"

618
00:27:34,840 --> 00:27:38,820
would all have exactly
the same representation.

619
00:27:38,820 --> 00:27:40,560
They have this
island of agreement.

620
00:27:40,560 --> 00:27:42,420
And that would be
a representation

621
00:27:42,420 --> 00:27:44,418
of a compound thing.

622
00:27:44,418 --> 00:27:45,960
And as you go up,
you're going to get

623
00:27:45,960 --> 00:27:47,668
these islands of
agreement that represent

624
00:27:47,668 --> 00:27:48,930
bigger and bigger things.

625
00:27:48,930 --> 00:27:51,570
And that's going to be a
much more useful kind of BERT

626
00:27:51,570 --> 00:27:55,620
because instead of taking
vectors that represent word

627
00:27:55,620 --> 00:27:58,800
fragments and then sort
of munging them together

628
00:27:58,800 --> 00:28:01,560
by taking the max
of each component,

629
00:28:01,560 --> 00:28:04,880
for example, which is
just a crazy thing to do,

630
00:28:04,880 --> 00:28:06,900
you would explicitly
as you're learning

631
00:28:06,900 --> 00:28:09,630
form representations
of larger parts

632
00:28:09,630 --> 00:28:12,120
in the part-whole hierarchy.

633
00:28:12,120 --> 00:28:12,620
OK.

634
00:28:12,620 --> 00:28:15,800

635
00:28:15,800 --> 00:28:19,280
So what are we going after
in GLOM is a particular kind

636
00:28:19,280 --> 00:28:20,750
of spatial coherence.

637
00:28:20,750 --> 00:28:23,600
That's more complicated than
the spatial coherence caused

638
00:28:23,600 --> 00:28:25,640
by the fact that
surfaces tend to be

639
00:28:25,640 --> 00:28:27,710
at the same depth
and same orientation

640
00:28:27,710 --> 00:28:30,160
in nearby patches of an image.

641
00:28:30,160 --> 00:28:34,180
We're going after the
spacial coherence that

642
00:28:34,180 --> 00:28:37,240
says that if you find
a mouth in an image

643
00:28:37,240 --> 00:28:38,980
and you find a nose
in an image and then

644
00:28:38,980 --> 00:28:41,260
the right spatial
relationship to make a face,

645
00:28:41,260 --> 00:28:44,230
then that's a particular
kind of coherence.

646
00:28:44,230 --> 00:28:46,840
And we want to go after
that unsupervised.

647
00:28:46,840 --> 00:28:50,470
And we want to discover that
kind of coherence in images.

648
00:28:50,470 --> 00:28:53,830

649
00:28:53,830 --> 00:28:56,770
So before I go into
more details about GLOM,

650
00:28:56,770 --> 00:28:57,940
I want a disclaimer.

651
00:28:57,940 --> 00:29:00,890

652
00:29:00,890 --> 00:29:03,050
For years, computer
vision treated vision

653
00:29:03,050 --> 00:29:06,060
as you've got a static
image, a uniform resolution,

654
00:29:06,060 --> 00:29:08,240
then you want to
say what's in it.

655
00:29:08,240 --> 00:29:10,280
That's not how vision
works in the real world.

656
00:29:10,280 --> 00:29:12,230
In the real world,
this is actually a loop

657
00:29:12,230 --> 00:29:14,740
where you decide where to look.

658
00:29:14,740 --> 00:29:18,250
If you're a person
or a robot, you

659
00:29:18,250 --> 00:29:20,410
better do that intelligently.

660
00:29:20,410 --> 00:29:25,150
And that gives you a
sample of the optic array.

661
00:29:25,150 --> 00:29:28,540
It turns the optic array,
the incoming light,

662
00:29:28,540 --> 00:29:30,697
into a retinal image.

663
00:29:30,697 --> 00:29:32,530
And on your retina, you
have high resolution

664
00:29:32,530 --> 00:29:35,780
in the middle and low
resolution around the edges.

665
00:29:35,780 --> 00:29:39,970
And so you're focusing
on particular details.

666
00:29:39,970 --> 00:29:43,210
And you never ever
process the whole image

667
00:29:43,210 --> 00:29:44,620
a uniform resolution.

668
00:29:44,620 --> 00:29:46,990
You're always focusing on
something and processing

669
00:29:46,990 --> 00:29:49,690
where you're fixating at high
resolution and everything

670
00:29:49,690 --> 00:29:53,600
else at much lower resolution,
particularly around the edges.

671
00:29:53,600 --> 00:29:56,500
So I'm going to ignore
all the complexity of how

672
00:29:56,500 --> 00:30:00,280
you decide where to look and all
the complexity of how you put

673
00:30:00,280 --> 00:30:03,280
together the information you
get from different fixations

674
00:30:03,280 --> 00:30:06,700
by saying, let's just talk
about the very first fixation

675
00:30:06,700 --> 00:30:07,900
of a novel image.

676
00:30:07,900 --> 00:30:09,910
So you look somewhere
and now what

677
00:30:09,910 --> 00:30:11,890
happens on that first fixation?

678
00:30:11,890 --> 00:30:13,720
We know that the same
hardware in the brain

679
00:30:13,720 --> 00:30:16,183
is going to be reused
for the next fixation.

680
00:30:16,183 --> 00:30:18,100
But let's just think
about the first fixation.

681
00:30:18,100 --> 00:30:20,780

682
00:30:20,780 --> 00:30:24,890
So finally here's a picture
of the architecture.

683
00:30:24,890 --> 00:30:30,500
And this is the architecture
for a single location,

684
00:30:30,500 --> 00:30:34,600
so like for a single
word fragment in BERT.

685
00:30:34,600 --> 00:30:38,590
And it shows you what's
happening for multiple frames.

686
00:30:38,590 --> 00:30:40,660
So GLOM is really
designed for video,

687
00:30:40,660 --> 00:30:43,660
but I only talk about
applying it to static images.

688
00:30:43,660 --> 00:30:45,520
Then you should think
of a static image

689
00:30:45,520 --> 00:30:48,520
as a very boring video in
which the frames are all

690
00:30:48,520 --> 00:30:51,030
the same as each other.

691
00:30:51,030 --> 00:30:53,930
So I'm showing you
three adjacent levels

692
00:30:53,930 --> 00:30:56,480
in the hierarchy.

693
00:30:56,480 --> 00:31:00,450
And I'm showing you
what happens over time.

694
00:31:00,450 --> 00:31:02,600
So if you look at
the middle level,

695
00:31:02,600 --> 00:31:06,200
maybe that's the sort
of major part level.

696
00:31:06,200 --> 00:31:09,370
And look at that box
that says "level L."

697
00:31:09,370 --> 00:31:15,720
And that's at frame four, so
the right hand level L box.

698
00:31:15,720 --> 00:31:18,160
And let's ask how the state
of that box-- the state

699
00:31:18,160 --> 00:31:20,445
of that embedding is determined.

700
00:31:20,445 --> 00:31:22,570
So inside the box, we're
going to get an embedding.

701
00:31:22,570 --> 00:31:25,150

702
00:31:25,150 --> 00:31:28,210
And the embedding is going to
be the representation of what's

703
00:31:28,210 --> 00:31:32,078
going on at the major part
level for that little patch

704
00:31:32,078 --> 00:31:32,620
of the image.

705
00:31:32,620 --> 00:31:35,500

706
00:31:35,500 --> 00:31:37,900
At level L the--
in this diagram,

707
00:31:37,900 --> 00:31:40,360
all of these
embeddings will always

708
00:31:40,360 --> 00:31:45,760
be devoted to the same
patch of the retinal image.

709
00:31:45,760 --> 00:31:47,860
OK.

710
00:31:47,860 --> 00:31:52,770
The level L embedding
on the right hand side.

711
00:31:52,770 --> 00:31:55,380
You can see there's three
things determining it there.

712
00:31:55,380 --> 00:31:57,140
There's the green arrow.

713
00:31:57,140 --> 00:31:59,785
And for static images, the
green arrow is rather boring.

714
00:31:59,785 --> 00:32:01,700
It's just saying you
should sort of be

715
00:32:01,700 --> 00:32:03,660
similar to the previous
state of level L.

716
00:32:03,660 --> 00:32:07,010
So it's just doing
temporal integration.

717
00:32:07,010 --> 00:32:12,070
The blue arrow is
actually a neural net

718
00:32:12,070 --> 00:32:14,162
with a couple of
hidden layers in it.

719
00:32:14,162 --> 00:32:15,870
I'm just showing you
the embeddings here,

720
00:32:15,870 --> 00:32:18,065
not all the layers
of the neural net.

721
00:32:18,065 --> 00:32:19,440
We need a couple
of hidden layers

722
00:32:19,440 --> 00:32:22,320
to do the coordinate
transforms that are required.

723
00:32:22,320 --> 00:32:25,860
And the blue arrow
is basically taking

724
00:32:25,860 --> 00:32:30,070
information of the level below
of the previous time step.

725
00:32:30,070 --> 00:32:32,910
So level L minus
1 on frame 3 might

726
00:32:32,910 --> 00:32:36,210
be representing that, I
think, I might be a nostril.

727
00:32:36,210 --> 00:32:38,190
Well, if you think you
might be a nostril,

728
00:32:38,190 --> 00:32:40,350
or you predicted
the next level up

729
00:32:40,350 --> 00:32:43,620
is a nose, what's
more if you have

730
00:32:43,620 --> 00:32:45,310
a coordinate frame
for the nostril,

731
00:32:45,310 --> 00:32:47,640
you can predict the
coordinate frame for the nose.

732
00:32:47,640 --> 00:32:50,010
Maybe not perfectly but
you have a pretty good idea

733
00:32:50,010 --> 00:32:52,980
of the orientation
position scale of the nose.

734
00:32:52,980 --> 00:32:58,530
So that bottom up
neural net is a net

735
00:32:58,530 --> 00:33:01,240
that can take any kind
of part or level in mind.

736
00:33:01,240 --> 00:33:03,270
So it can take a nostril,
but it could also

737
00:33:03,270 --> 00:33:05,490
take a steering wheel
and predict the car

738
00:33:05,490 --> 00:33:10,850
from the steering wheel
and predict what you've

739
00:33:10,850 --> 00:33:14,420
got at the next level up.

740
00:33:14,420 --> 00:33:17,850
The red arrow is a
top down neural net.

741
00:33:17,850 --> 00:33:25,487
So the red arrow is predicting
the nose from the whole face.

742
00:33:25,487 --> 00:33:27,320
And, again, it has a
couple of hidden layers

743
00:33:27,320 --> 00:33:29,150
to do coordinate transforms.

744
00:33:29,150 --> 00:33:32,210
Because if you know the
corner frame of the face

745
00:33:32,210 --> 00:33:34,895
and you know the relationship
between a face and a nose,

746
00:33:34,895 --> 00:33:36,770
and that's going to be
in the weights of that

747
00:33:36,770 --> 00:33:38,420
top down neural net.

748
00:33:38,420 --> 00:33:41,360
Then you can predict
that it's a nose

749
00:33:41,360 --> 00:33:43,490
and what the pose
of the nose is.

750
00:33:43,490 --> 00:33:46,220
And that's all going to be in
activities in that embedding

751
00:33:46,220 --> 00:33:48,308
vector.

752
00:33:48,308 --> 00:33:49,880
OK.

753
00:33:49,880 --> 00:33:53,870
All of that is what's going
on in one column of hardware.

754
00:33:53,870 --> 00:33:57,120
That's all about a specific
patch of the image.

755
00:33:57,120 --> 00:34:00,770
So that's very, very like
what's going on for one word

756
00:34:00,770 --> 00:34:02,000
fragmenting BERT.

757
00:34:02,000 --> 00:34:04,040
You have all these
levels of representation.

758
00:34:04,040 --> 00:34:08,620

759
00:34:08,620 --> 00:34:11,050
It's a bit confusing exactly
what the relation of this

760
00:34:11,050 --> 00:34:11,679
is to BERT.

761
00:34:11,679 --> 00:34:13,639
And I'll give you the
reference to a long Arxiv

762
00:34:13,639 --> 00:34:15,639
paper at the end that has
a whole section on how

763
00:34:15,639 --> 00:34:17,409
this relates to BERT.

764
00:34:17,409 --> 00:34:21,989
But it's confusing because
this has time steps.

765
00:34:21,989 --> 00:34:24,540
And that makes it
all more complicated.

766
00:34:24,540 --> 00:34:26,639
OK.

767
00:34:26,639 --> 00:34:28,739
So those are three
things that determine

768
00:34:28,739 --> 00:34:30,570
the level of
embedding, but there's

769
00:34:30,570 --> 00:34:35,730
one fourth thing, which is
in black at the bottom there.

770
00:34:35,730 --> 00:34:39,610
And that's the only way in which
different locations interact.

771
00:34:39,610 --> 00:34:43,500
And that's a very simplified
form of a transformer.

772
00:34:43,500 --> 00:34:46,199
If you take a
transformer as in BERT

773
00:34:46,199 --> 00:34:49,560
and you say, let's make
the embeddings and the keys

774
00:34:49,560 --> 00:34:53,010
and the queries and the values
all be the same as each other,

775
00:34:53,010 --> 00:34:55,550
you just have this one vector.

776
00:34:55,550 --> 00:34:59,860
So now all you're trying
to do is make the level L

777
00:34:59,860 --> 00:35:03,820
embedding in one column
be the same as the level

778
00:35:03,820 --> 00:35:08,473
L embedding in nearby columns,
but it's going to be gated

779
00:35:08,473 --> 00:35:10,640
and then you're going to
try and make it be the same

780
00:35:10,640 --> 00:35:12,905
if it's already quite similar.

781
00:35:12,905 --> 00:35:15,690

782
00:35:15,690 --> 00:35:18,320
So here's how the
attention works.

783
00:35:18,320 --> 00:35:22,930
You take the level L embedding
in location x, that's Lx.

784
00:35:22,930 --> 00:35:25,310
And you take the level L
embedding in a nearby location

785
00:35:25,310 --> 00:35:27,320
y, that's Ly.

786
00:35:27,320 --> 00:35:29,640
You take the scalar product.

787
00:35:29,640 --> 00:35:32,870
You exponentiate
and you normalize.

788
00:35:32,870 --> 00:35:35,360
In other words,
you do a softmax.

789
00:35:35,360 --> 00:35:40,340
And that gives you the
weight to use in your desire

790
00:35:40,340 --> 00:35:45,570
to make Lx be the same as Ly.

791
00:35:45,570 --> 00:35:51,280
So the input produced by this--

792
00:35:51,280 --> 00:35:54,955
from neighbors is an attention
weighted average of the level

793
00:35:54,955 --> 00:35:57,570
L embeddings of nearby columns.

794
00:35:57,570 --> 00:35:59,550
And that's an extra
input that you get.

795
00:35:59,550 --> 00:36:02,348
It's trying to make you
agree with nearby things.

796
00:36:02,348 --> 00:36:04,140
And that's what's going
to cause you to get

797
00:36:04,140 --> 00:36:05,430
these islands of agreement.

798
00:36:05,430 --> 00:36:10,560

799
00:36:10,560 --> 00:36:14,095
So back to this
picture, I think--

800
00:36:14,095 --> 00:36:17,280

801
00:36:17,280 --> 00:36:21,150
yeah, this is what
we'd like to see.

802
00:36:21,150 --> 00:36:25,380
And the reason we get those--

803
00:36:25,380 --> 00:36:28,350
that big island of agreement
at the object level

804
00:36:28,350 --> 00:36:30,570
is because we're trying
to get agreement there.

805
00:36:30,570 --> 00:36:34,050
We're trying to learn
the coordinate transform

806
00:36:34,050 --> 00:36:37,440
from the red arrows to the level
above and from the green arrows

807
00:36:37,440 --> 00:36:41,470
to the level above such
that we get agreement.

808
00:36:41,470 --> 00:36:41,970
OK.

809
00:36:41,970 --> 00:36:45,430

810
00:36:45,430 --> 00:36:47,830
Now, one thing we
need to worry about

811
00:36:47,830 --> 00:36:53,040
is that the difficult
thing in perception it's

812
00:36:53,040 --> 00:36:54,270
not so bad in language.

813
00:36:54,270 --> 00:36:56,670
It's probably worse
than visual perception,

814
00:36:56,670 --> 00:36:58,590
is there's a lot of ambiguity.

815
00:36:58,590 --> 00:37:00,040
If I'm looking at
a line drawing,

816
00:37:00,040 --> 00:37:02,320
for example, I see a circle.

817
00:37:02,320 --> 00:37:04,627
Well, the circle could be
the right eye of a face,

818
00:37:04,627 --> 00:37:06,210
or it could be the
left eye of a face,

819
00:37:06,210 --> 00:37:07,500
or it could be the
front wheel of a car

820
00:37:07,500 --> 00:37:08,700
or the back wheel of a car.

821
00:37:08,700 --> 00:37:11,520
There's all sorts of things
that that circle could be.

822
00:37:11,520 --> 00:37:14,850
And we'd like to
disambiguate the circle.

823
00:37:14,850 --> 00:37:18,180
And there's a long line
of work using things

824
00:37:18,180 --> 00:37:19,710
like Markov random fields.

825
00:37:19,710 --> 00:37:22,140
Here, we need a variation on
a Markov random field, which

826
00:37:22,140 --> 00:37:24,360
I call a "transformational
random field"

827
00:37:24,360 --> 00:37:27,328
because the interaction
between, for example,

828
00:37:27,328 --> 00:37:29,370
something that might be
an eye and something that

829
00:37:29,370 --> 00:37:34,707
might be a mouth needs to be
gated by corner transforms.

830
00:37:34,707 --> 00:37:36,540
Let's take a nose and
a mouth because that's

831
00:37:36,540 --> 00:37:37,830
my standard thing.

832
00:37:37,830 --> 00:37:41,050
If you take something that might
be a nose and you want to ask,

833
00:37:41,050 --> 00:37:44,850
does anybody out there
support the idea of the nose?

834
00:37:44,850 --> 00:37:49,050
Well, what you'd like to do
is send to everything nearby

835
00:37:49,050 --> 00:37:53,580
a message saying, do
you have the right kind

836
00:37:53,580 --> 00:37:56,640
of pose and right
kind of identity

837
00:37:56,640 --> 00:37:59,340
to support the idea
that I am a nose?

838
00:37:59,340 --> 00:38:01,350
And so you'd like, for
example, to send out

839
00:38:01,350 --> 00:38:04,200
a message from the nose.

840
00:38:04,200 --> 00:38:07,200
You'd send out a message to
all nearby locations saying,

841
00:38:07,200 --> 00:38:09,510
does anybody have a
mouth with the pose

842
00:38:09,510 --> 00:38:13,350
that I predict by taking the
pose of the nose multiplying

843
00:38:13,350 --> 00:38:15,960
by the coordinate transformed
between the nose and the mouth?

844
00:38:15,960 --> 00:38:18,000
And now, I can predict
the pose of the mouth.

845
00:38:18,000 --> 00:38:20,160
Is there anybody out
there with that pose who

846
00:38:20,160 --> 00:38:22,130
thinks there might be a mouth?

847
00:38:22,130 --> 00:38:24,463
And I think you can see you're
going to have to send out

848
00:38:24,463 --> 00:38:26,333
a lot of different messages.

849
00:38:26,333 --> 00:38:28,500
For each kind of other thing
that might support you,

850
00:38:28,500 --> 00:38:30,208
you're going to send
a different message.

851
00:38:30,208 --> 00:38:35,040
So you're going to need a
multi-headed transformer.

852
00:38:35,040 --> 00:38:37,920
And it's going to be doing
these coordinate transforms.

853
00:38:37,920 --> 00:38:40,980
And you have to coordinate
transform the inverse transform

854
00:38:40,980 --> 00:38:43,920
on the way back because
if the mouth supports you,

855
00:38:43,920 --> 00:38:46,620
what it needs to
support is a nose,

856
00:38:46,620 --> 00:38:49,723
not with the pose of the mouth
but with the appropriate pose.

857
00:38:49,723 --> 00:38:51,390
So that's going to
get very complicated.

858
00:38:51,390 --> 00:38:53,348
You're going to have n
squared interactions all

859
00:38:53,348 --> 00:38:54,630
with coordinate transforms.

860
00:38:54,630 --> 00:38:56,297
There's another way
of doing it and it's

861
00:38:56,297 --> 00:38:58,990
much simpler that's
called a Hough transform.

862
00:38:58,990 --> 00:39:01,030
At least, it's
much simpler if you

863
00:39:01,030 --> 00:39:02,950
have a way of
representing ambiguity.

864
00:39:02,950 --> 00:39:06,050

865
00:39:06,050 --> 00:39:08,530
So instead of these
direct interactions

866
00:39:08,530 --> 00:39:12,540
between parts like a nose and a
mouth, what you're going to do

867
00:39:12,540 --> 00:39:17,510
is you're going to make each
of the parts predict the whole.

868
00:39:17,510 --> 00:39:19,910
So the nose can
protect the face,

869
00:39:19,910 --> 00:39:21,830
but it can predict
the pose of the face.

870
00:39:21,830 --> 00:39:24,530
And the mouth can
also predict the face.

871
00:39:24,530 --> 00:39:26,750
Now, these will be in
different columns of GLOM.

872
00:39:26,750 --> 00:39:28,130
But in one column
of GLOM, you'll

873
00:39:28,130 --> 00:39:29,960
have a nose predicting a face.

874
00:39:29,960 --> 00:39:33,170
In a nearby column, you'll
have a mouth predicting a face.

875
00:39:33,170 --> 00:39:35,820
And those two faces
should be the same

876
00:39:35,820 --> 00:39:38,740
if this really is a face.

877
00:39:38,740 --> 00:39:40,690
So when you do this
attention weighted

878
00:39:40,690 --> 00:39:43,090
averaging with nearby
things, what you're doing

879
00:39:43,090 --> 00:39:48,340
is you're getting
confirmation that the support

880
00:39:48,340 --> 00:39:49,840
for the hypothesis you got.

881
00:39:49,840 --> 00:39:51,760
I mean, suppose for one
column the hypothesis

882
00:39:51,760 --> 00:39:53,770
is a face with this pose.

883
00:39:53,770 --> 00:39:56,110
That gets supported
by nearby columns

884
00:39:56,110 --> 00:39:59,890
that derive the very
same embedding from quite

885
00:39:59,890 --> 00:40:00,590
different data.

886
00:40:00,590 --> 00:40:02,590
One derived it from the
nose, and one derived it

887
00:40:02,590 --> 00:40:05,310
from the mouth.

888
00:40:05,310 --> 00:40:08,670
And this doesn't require
any dynamic routine

889
00:40:08,670 --> 00:40:10,530
because the
embeddings are always

890
00:40:10,530 --> 00:40:12,930
referring to what's going
on in the same small patch

891
00:40:12,930 --> 00:40:14,220
of the image.

892
00:40:14,220 --> 00:40:16,500
Within a column
there's no routing.

893
00:40:16,500 --> 00:40:20,080
And between columns, there's
something a bit like routing,

894
00:40:20,080 --> 00:40:22,780
but it's just the standard
transformer kind of attention.

895
00:40:22,780 --> 00:40:25,155
You're just trying to agree
with things that are similar.

896
00:40:25,155 --> 00:40:28,910

897
00:40:28,910 --> 00:40:32,330
OK, so that's how GLOM
was meant to work.

898
00:40:32,330 --> 00:40:35,190
And the big problem is--

899
00:40:35,190 --> 00:40:37,425
that if I see a circle,
it might be a left eye,

900
00:40:37,425 --> 00:40:38,550
or it might be a right eye.

901
00:40:38,550 --> 00:40:40,605
It might be a front
wheel of the car

902
00:40:40,605 --> 00:40:42,300
or it might be the
back wheel of a car.

903
00:40:42,300 --> 00:40:44,520
Because my embedding
for a particular patch

904
00:40:44,520 --> 00:40:47,490
at a particular level has to
be able to represent anything,

905
00:40:47,490 --> 00:40:49,800
when I get an
ambiguous thing, I have

906
00:40:49,800 --> 00:40:52,140
to deal with all these
possibilities of what whole

907
00:40:52,140 --> 00:40:54,180
it might be part of.

908
00:40:54,180 --> 00:40:56,430
So instead of trying
to resolve ambiguity

909
00:40:56,430 --> 00:40:59,460
at the part level, what I can
do is jump to the next level up

910
00:40:59,460 --> 00:41:02,040
and resolve the ambiguity
there just by seeing if things

911
00:41:02,040 --> 00:41:05,610
are the same, which is an
easier way to resolve ambiguity.

912
00:41:05,610 --> 00:41:08,220
But the cost of
that is I have to be

913
00:41:08,220 --> 00:41:10,460
able to represent
all the ambiguity I

914
00:41:10,460 --> 00:41:13,190
get at the next level up.

915
00:41:13,190 --> 00:41:14,660
Now, it turns out
you can do that.

916
00:41:14,660 --> 00:41:19,100
We've done a little toy
example where you can actually

917
00:41:19,100 --> 00:41:23,300
preserve this ambiguity,
but it's difficult.

918
00:41:23,300 --> 00:41:26,700
It's the kind of thing
neural nets are good at.

919
00:41:26,700 --> 00:41:31,160
So if you think about the
embedding of the next level up,

920
00:41:31,160 --> 00:41:34,340
you got a whole bunch of
neurons whose activities

921
00:41:34,340 --> 00:41:36,260
are that embedding.

922
00:41:36,260 --> 00:41:40,122
And you want to represent a
highly multimodal distribution,

923
00:41:40,122 --> 00:41:42,080
like, it might be a car
with this pose or a car

924
00:41:42,080 --> 00:41:44,538
with that pose or a face with
this pose or a face with that

925
00:41:44,538 --> 00:41:45,410
pose.

926
00:41:45,410 --> 00:41:47,120
All of these are
possible predictions

927
00:41:47,120 --> 00:41:49,380
for finding a circle.

928
00:41:49,380 --> 00:41:52,640
And so you have to
represent all that.

929
00:41:52,640 --> 00:41:54,920
And the question is,
can neural nets do that?

930
00:41:54,920 --> 00:41:56,510
But I think the way
they must be doing

931
00:41:56,510 --> 00:42:01,320
it is each neuron
in the embedding

932
00:42:01,320 --> 00:42:04,500
stands for an unnormalized
log probability

933
00:42:04,500 --> 00:42:08,010
distribution over this huge
space of possible identities

934
00:42:08,010 --> 00:42:10,915
and possible poses, the sort
of cross product of identities

935
00:42:10,915 --> 00:42:11,415
and poses.

936
00:42:11,415 --> 00:42:14,220

937
00:42:14,220 --> 00:42:17,670
And so the neuron is this
rather vague log probability

938
00:42:17,670 --> 00:42:20,270
distribution over that space.

939
00:42:20,270 --> 00:42:22,780
And when you activate the
neuron, what it's saying

940
00:42:22,780 --> 00:42:25,780
is add in that log
probability distribution

941
00:42:25,780 --> 00:42:27,478
to what you've already got.

942
00:42:27,478 --> 00:42:29,770
And so now if you have a
whole bunch of log probability

943
00:42:29,770 --> 00:42:33,950
distributions and you
add them all together,

944
00:42:33,950 --> 00:42:38,677
you can get a much more peaky
log probability distribution.

945
00:42:38,677 --> 00:42:41,260
And when you exponentiate, you
get a probability distribution.

946
00:42:41,260 --> 00:42:44,620
It gets very peaky
and so very vague

947
00:42:44,620 --> 00:42:50,230
basis functions in this joint
space of pose and identity

948
00:42:50,230 --> 00:42:51,280
and basis functions.

949
00:42:51,280 --> 00:42:53,590
And the log probability
in that space

950
00:42:53,590 --> 00:42:58,460
can be combined to
produce sharp conclusions.

951
00:42:58,460 --> 00:43:02,350
So I think that's how neurons
are representing things.

952
00:43:02,350 --> 00:43:04,870
Most people think
about neurons as--

953
00:43:04,870 --> 00:43:08,510
they think about the thing
that they're representing

954
00:43:08,510 --> 00:43:12,980
but obviously in perception you
have to deal with uncertainty.

955
00:43:12,980 --> 00:43:16,640
And so neurons have to
be good at representing

956
00:43:16,640 --> 00:43:18,680
multimodal distributions.

957
00:43:18,680 --> 00:43:20,180
And this is the
only way I can think

958
00:43:20,180 --> 00:43:23,340
of that's good at doing it.

959
00:43:23,340 --> 00:43:25,177
That's a rather weak argument.

960
00:43:25,177 --> 00:43:26,760
But it's the argument
that led Chomsky

961
00:43:26,760 --> 00:43:29,280
to believe that language wasn't
learned because he couldn't

962
00:43:29,280 --> 00:43:31,980
think of how it was learned.

963
00:43:31,980 --> 00:43:34,770
My view is neurons must be using
this representation because I

964
00:43:34,770 --> 00:43:36,478
can't think of any
other way of doing it.

965
00:43:36,478 --> 00:43:40,350

966
00:43:40,350 --> 00:43:43,138
OK, I just said all that
because I got ahead of myself

967
00:43:43,138 --> 00:43:44,055
because I got excited.

968
00:43:44,055 --> 00:43:48,505

969
00:43:48,505 --> 00:43:51,130
Now, the reason you can get away
with this, the reason you have

970
00:43:51,130 --> 00:43:53,800
these very vague distributions
in the unnormalized log

971
00:43:53,800 --> 00:43:58,390
probability space is because
these neurons are all

972
00:43:58,390 --> 00:44:00,760
dedicated to a small
patch of image,

973
00:44:00,760 --> 00:44:02,410
and they're all
trying to represent

974
00:44:02,410 --> 00:44:05,420
the thing that's happening
in that patch of image.

975
00:44:05,420 --> 00:44:07,690
So you're only trying
to represent one thing.

976
00:44:07,690 --> 00:44:10,812
You're not trying to represent
some set of possible objects.

977
00:44:10,812 --> 00:44:13,270
If you're trying to represent
some set of possible objects,

978
00:44:13,270 --> 00:44:14,920
you have a horrible
binding problem,

979
00:44:14,920 --> 00:44:17,200
and you couldn't use these
very vague distributions.

980
00:44:17,200 --> 00:44:20,890
But so long as you know
that, all of these neurons,

981
00:44:20,890 --> 00:44:24,520
all of the active neurons
refer to the same thing.

982
00:44:24,520 --> 00:44:26,710
Then you can do
the intersection.

983
00:44:26,710 --> 00:44:28,960
You can add the log probability
distributions together

984
00:44:28,960 --> 00:44:31,600
and intersect the sets
of things they represent.

985
00:44:31,600 --> 00:44:34,280

986
00:44:34,280 --> 00:44:35,300
OK.

987
00:44:35,300 --> 00:44:36,560
I'm getting near the end.

988
00:44:36,560 --> 00:44:38,960
How would you train
a system like this?

989
00:44:38,960 --> 00:44:41,630
Well, obviously, you could
train it the way you train BERT.

990
00:44:41,630 --> 00:44:45,200
You could do deep
end-to-end training.

991
00:44:45,200 --> 00:44:48,860
And for GLOM, what
that will consist of

992
00:44:48,860 --> 00:44:55,700
and the way we trained the toy
example is you take an image.

993
00:44:55,700 --> 00:45:00,580
You leave out some
patches of the image.

994
00:45:00,580 --> 00:45:04,890
You then let GLOM settle
down for about 10 iterations.

995
00:45:04,890 --> 00:45:09,000
And it's trying to fill
in the lowest level

996
00:45:09,000 --> 00:45:13,130
representation of what's in
the image, the lowest level

997
00:45:13,130 --> 00:45:14,540
embeddings.

998
00:45:14,540 --> 00:45:17,370
And it fills them in wrong.

999
00:45:17,370 --> 00:45:19,333
And so you now back
propagate that error.

1000
00:45:19,333 --> 00:45:20,750
When you're back
propagating, it's

1001
00:45:20,750 --> 00:45:22,520
through time in this network.

1002
00:45:22,520 --> 00:45:26,892
So it will also back propagate
up and down through the levels.

1003
00:45:26,892 --> 00:45:28,850
So you're basically just
doing back propagation

1004
00:45:28,850 --> 00:45:34,220
through time of the error due to
filling in things incorrectly.

1005
00:45:34,220 --> 00:45:36,950
That's basically
how BERT is trained.

1006
00:45:36,950 --> 00:45:39,570
And you could train
GLOM the same way.

1007
00:45:39,570 --> 00:45:43,160
But I also want to include
an extra bit in the training

1008
00:45:43,160 --> 00:45:44,150
to encourage islands.

1009
00:45:44,150 --> 00:45:47,620

1010
00:45:47,620 --> 00:45:52,810
We want to encourage big
islands of identical vectors

1011
00:45:52,810 --> 00:45:54,550
at high levels.

1012
00:45:54,550 --> 00:45:58,230
And you can do that by
using constrastive learning.

1013
00:45:58,230 --> 00:46:04,700
So if you think how at
the next time step--

1014
00:46:04,700 --> 00:46:07,670
if you think how an
embedding is determined,

1015
00:46:07,670 --> 00:46:11,600
it's determined by combining
a whole bunch of different

1016
00:46:11,600 --> 00:46:12,717
factors--

1017
00:46:12,717 --> 00:46:14,300
what was going on
in the previous time

1018
00:46:14,300 --> 00:46:16,970
step at this level
of representation

1019
00:46:16,970 --> 00:46:19,340
in this location,
what was going on

1020
00:46:19,340 --> 00:46:22,520
at the the previous time
step in this location

1021
00:46:22,520 --> 00:46:26,030
but at the next level
down or the next level up,

1022
00:46:26,030 --> 00:46:28,820
and also what was going
on at the previous time

1023
00:46:28,820 --> 00:46:33,410
step at nearby locations
at the same level.

1024
00:46:33,410 --> 00:46:36,182
And the weighted average
of all those things

1025
00:46:36,182 --> 00:46:37,640
are called a
"consensus" embedding.

1026
00:46:37,640 --> 00:46:40,570
And that's what you use
for the next embedding.

1027
00:46:40,570 --> 00:46:42,400
And I think you can see that.

1028
00:46:42,400 --> 00:46:45,010
If we try to make the
bottom up neural net

1029
00:46:45,010 --> 00:46:47,140
or the top down
neural net, if we

1030
00:46:47,140 --> 00:46:50,900
try to make the predictions
agree with the consensus,

1031
00:46:50,900 --> 00:46:54,200
the consensus has
folded in information

1032
00:46:54,200 --> 00:46:59,000
from nearby locations that
already roughly agree because

1033
00:46:59,000 --> 00:47:01,168
of the attention weighting.

1034
00:47:01,168 --> 00:47:02,710
And so by trying to
make the top down

1035
00:47:02,710 --> 00:47:05,890
and bottom up neural networks
agree with the consensus,

1036
00:47:05,890 --> 00:47:07,840
you're trying to make
them agree with what's

1037
00:47:07,840 --> 00:47:10,520
going on in nearby
locations that are similar,

1038
00:47:10,520 --> 00:47:12,475
and so you'll be training
it to form islands.

1039
00:47:12,475 --> 00:47:17,750

1040
00:47:17,750 --> 00:47:19,810
This is more interesting
to neuroscientists

1041
00:47:19,810 --> 00:47:21,830
than to people of
your natural language,

1042
00:47:21,830 --> 00:47:25,270
so I'm going to ignore that.

1043
00:47:25,270 --> 00:47:27,790
You might think
it's wasteful to be

1044
00:47:27,790 --> 00:47:31,494
replicating all these
embeddings at the object level.

1045
00:47:31,494 --> 00:47:33,077
So the idea is at
the the object level

1046
00:47:33,077 --> 00:47:34,990
there would be a large
number of patches

1047
00:47:34,990 --> 00:47:38,150
that all have exactly the
same vector representation.

1048
00:47:38,150 --> 00:47:39,650
And that seemed like a waste.

1049
00:47:39,650 --> 00:47:41,840
But actually biology is
full of things like that.

1050
00:47:41,840 --> 00:47:44,540
All your cells have
exactly the same DNA.

1051
00:47:44,540 --> 00:47:47,240
And all the parts
of an organ have

1052
00:47:47,240 --> 00:47:49,790
pretty much the same vector
of protein expressions.

1053
00:47:49,790 --> 00:47:51,320
So there's lots of
replication that

1054
00:47:51,320 --> 00:47:55,252
goes on to keep things local.

1055
00:47:55,252 --> 00:47:56,210
And it's the same here.

1056
00:47:56,210 --> 00:47:59,600
And actually that
replication is very useful

1057
00:47:59,600 --> 00:48:01,790
when you're settling
on an interpretation.

1058
00:48:01,790 --> 00:48:03,940
Because before you
settle down, you

1059
00:48:03,940 --> 00:48:05,690
don't know which things
should be the same

1060
00:48:05,690 --> 00:48:06,840
as which other things.

1061
00:48:06,840 --> 00:48:08,690
So having separate
vectors in each location

1062
00:48:08,690 --> 00:48:11,270
to represent what's going
on there at the object level

1063
00:48:11,270 --> 00:48:14,030
gives you the flexibility to
gradually segment things as you

1064
00:48:14,030 --> 00:48:17,670
settle down in a sensible way.

1065
00:48:17,670 --> 00:48:19,680
It allows you to
hedge your bets.

1066
00:48:19,680 --> 00:48:22,230
And what you're doing is
not quite like clustering.

1067
00:48:22,230 --> 00:48:25,410
You're creating clusters
of identical vectors

1068
00:48:25,410 --> 00:48:28,080
rather than discovering
clusters in fixed data.

1069
00:48:28,080 --> 00:48:30,747
So clustering, you're given
the data, and it's fixed,

1070
00:48:30,747 --> 00:48:31,830
and you find the clusters.

1071
00:48:31,830 --> 00:48:38,040
Here, the embeddings at every
level they vary over time.

1072
00:48:38,040 --> 00:48:40,410
They're determined by the
top down and bottom up inputs

1073
00:48:40,410 --> 00:48:42,490
and by inputs coming
from nearby locations.

1074
00:48:42,490 --> 00:48:44,910
So what you're doing
is forming clusters

1075
00:48:44,910 --> 00:48:47,460
rather than discovering
them in fixed data.

1076
00:48:47,460 --> 00:48:49,470
And that's got a
somewhat different flavor

1077
00:48:49,470 --> 00:48:51,120
and can settle down faster.

1078
00:48:51,120 --> 00:48:55,720

1079
00:48:55,720 --> 00:48:58,360
And one other advantage
of this replication

1080
00:48:58,360 --> 00:49:01,600
is what you don't
want is to have

1081
00:49:01,600 --> 00:49:03,640
much more work in
your transformer

1082
00:49:03,640 --> 00:49:06,010
as you go to higher levels.

1083
00:49:06,010 --> 00:49:08,985
But you do need longer range
interactions at higher levels.

1084
00:49:08,985 --> 00:49:10,360
Presumably for
the lowest levels,

1085
00:49:10,360 --> 00:49:12,100
you want fairly
short-range interactions

1086
00:49:12,100 --> 00:49:13,300
in your transformer.

1087
00:49:13,300 --> 00:49:14,583
And they could be dense.

1088
00:49:14,583 --> 00:49:16,750
As you go to high levels,
you want much longer range

1089
00:49:16,750 --> 00:49:20,290
interactions so you
could make them sparse.

1090
00:49:20,290 --> 00:49:25,810
And people have done things
like that for BERT like systems.

1091
00:49:25,810 --> 00:49:28,660
Here, it's easy to make
them sparse because you're

1092
00:49:28,660 --> 00:49:30,310
expecting big islands.

1093
00:49:30,310 --> 00:49:34,870
So all you need to do is see
one patch of a big island

1094
00:49:34,870 --> 00:49:37,150
to know what the vector
representation of that island

1095
00:49:37,150 --> 00:49:37,960
is.

1096
00:49:37,960 --> 00:49:39,610
And so sparse
representations will

1097
00:49:39,610 --> 00:49:42,250
work much better if you have
these big islands of agreement

1098
00:49:42,250 --> 00:49:42,940
as you go up.

1099
00:49:42,940 --> 00:49:45,070
So the idea is you have
longer range and sparser

1100
00:49:45,070 --> 00:49:46,250
connections as you go up.

1101
00:49:46,250 --> 00:49:50,440
So the amount of computation
is the same at every level.

1102
00:49:50,440 --> 00:49:55,300
And just to summarize,
I showed how

1103
00:49:55,300 --> 00:49:57,550
to combine three important
advances of neural networks

1104
00:49:57,550 --> 00:49:58,210
in GLOM.

1105
00:49:58,210 --> 00:50:01,180
I didn't actually talk
about neural fields.

1106
00:50:01,180 --> 00:50:04,050
And that's important for
the top down network.

1107
00:50:04,050 --> 00:50:05,800
Maybe since I've got
two minutes to spare,

1108
00:50:05,800 --> 00:50:08,300
I'm going to go back and mention
neural fields very briefly.

1109
00:50:08,300 --> 00:50:13,770

1110
00:50:13,770 --> 00:50:16,950
Yeah, when I trained that
top down neural network--

1111
00:50:16,950 --> 00:50:18,790
and I have a problem.

1112
00:50:18,790 --> 00:50:23,530
And the problem is if you
look at those red arrows

1113
00:50:23,530 --> 00:50:28,060
and those green arrows,
they're quite different.

1114
00:50:28,060 --> 00:50:31,170
But if you look at the level
above the object level,

1115
00:50:31,170 --> 00:50:34,390
all those vectors are the same.

1116
00:50:34,390 --> 00:50:36,990
And, of course, in
an engineered system,

1117
00:50:36,990 --> 00:50:39,610
I want to replicate the
neural nets in every location.

1118
00:50:39,610 --> 00:50:42,210
So I use exactly the same top
down and bottom up neural nets

1119
00:50:42,210 --> 00:50:43,870
everywhere.

1120
00:50:43,870 --> 00:50:47,040
And so the question is,
how can the same neural net

1121
00:50:47,040 --> 00:50:50,692
be given a black arrow and
sometimes produce a red arrow

1122
00:50:50,692 --> 00:50:52,650
and sometimes produce a
green arrow, which have

1123
00:50:52,650 --> 00:50:54,540
quite different orientations?

1124
00:50:54,540 --> 00:50:57,150
How can it produce
a nose where there's

1125
00:50:57,150 --> 00:51:00,330
a nose and a mouth where there's
a mouth even though the face

1126
00:51:00,330 --> 00:51:02,380
vector is the same everywhere?

1127
00:51:02,380 --> 00:51:06,330
And the answer is the top down
neural network doesn't just

1128
00:51:06,330 --> 00:51:09,150
get the face vector.

1129
00:51:09,150 --> 00:51:12,720
It also gets the location
of the patch, which

1130
00:51:12,720 --> 00:51:16,220
is producing the power vector.

1131
00:51:16,220 --> 00:51:17,720
So the three patches
that should get

1132
00:51:17,720 --> 00:51:19,428
the red vector are
different from three--

1133
00:51:19,428 --> 00:51:21,890
have different locations
from the three patches

1134
00:51:21,890 --> 00:51:23,732
that should get
the green vector.

1135
00:51:23,732 --> 00:51:25,232
So if I use the
neural network and I

1136
00:51:25,232 --> 00:51:29,040
guess the location as input as
well, here's what it can do.

1137
00:51:29,040 --> 00:51:32,030
It can take the pose, that's
encoded in that black vector,

1138
00:51:32,030 --> 00:51:34,210
the pose of the face.

1139
00:51:34,210 --> 00:51:39,390
It can take the location in the
image for which it's predicting

1140
00:51:39,390 --> 00:51:41,880
the vector of the level below.

1141
00:51:41,880 --> 00:51:44,150
And the pose is relative
to the image too.

1142
00:51:44,150 --> 00:51:45,650
So knowing the
location in the image

1143
00:51:45,650 --> 00:51:47,317
and knowing the poses
of the whole face,

1144
00:51:47,317 --> 00:51:49,140
it can figure out which
bit of the face it

1145
00:51:49,140 --> 00:51:52,993
needs to predict
at that location.

1146
00:51:52,993 --> 00:51:54,660
And so in one location,
you can predict,

1147
00:51:54,660 --> 00:51:56,910
OK, there should be nose
there, and it gives you

1148
00:51:56,910 --> 00:51:57,780
the right vector.

1149
00:51:57,780 --> 00:52:00,840
In another location, it can
predict from where that image

1150
00:52:00,840 --> 00:52:02,640
patch is there should
be a nose there,

1151
00:52:02,640 --> 00:52:04,720
so it can give you
the green arrow.

1152
00:52:04,720 --> 00:52:06,610
So you can get the same
vector at the level

1153
00:52:06,610 --> 00:52:08,280
above to predict
different vectors

1154
00:52:08,280 --> 00:52:10,590
in different places of the
level below by giving it

1155
00:52:10,590 --> 00:52:12,057
the place that it's
predicting for.

1156
00:52:12,057 --> 00:52:13,890
And that's what's going
on in neural fields.

1157
00:52:13,890 --> 00:52:21,230

1158
00:52:21,230 --> 00:52:23,500
OK, now, this was quite
a complicated talk.

1159
00:52:23,500 --> 00:52:25,600
There's a long paper
about it on Arxiv

1160
00:52:25,600 --> 00:52:28,270
that goes into much more detail.

1161
00:52:28,270 --> 00:52:30,850
And you could view this talk
as just an encouragement

1162
00:52:30,850 --> 00:52:32,560
to read that paper.

1163
00:52:32,560 --> 00:52:34,750
And I'm done, exactly on time.

1164
00:52:34,750 --> 00:52:39,450

1165
00:52:39,450 --> 00:52:39,950
Thank you.

1166
00:52:39,950 --> 00:52:40,783
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

1167
00:52:40,783 --> 00:52:41,400
Thanks a lot.

1168
00:52:41,400 --> 00:52:42,950
Yeah.

1169
00:52:42,950 --> 00:52:48,000

